id: pm-gh1749
title: "GH-1749: adding a secret-guard rule breaks typed consumers' exhaustive Record<SecretGuardFinding['rule'], ...> policy maps; offer a stable category per rule and a default-plus-overrides policy helper"
description: "GitHub issue 1749 (fleet report): extensions that type a per-rule secret guard policy as an exhaustive Record over SecretGuardFinding['rule'] failed tsc with TS2741 on the 2026.11.13 bump, when the rule union gained credential_key_assignment. The detector was additive, but for exhaustive typed consumers it is a breaking type change that the release notes did not flag. Proposed: export a stable category per rule (for example credential, identity, path, network) and a policy helper such as a default decision plus partial overrides with a decide function, so a new credential rule inherits refuse without breaking compilation; and list new rule literals under a typed-consumers note in release notes. Related: the secret-guard family pm-hw00c2 (credential-key reference literals) and pm-x07qz2 (property names) changed the same finding type."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[5]: api-design,"gh:1749",sdk,secret-guard,typed-consumers
created_at: "2026-11-17T21:58:37.940Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-18T19:12:59.906Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-18T11:25:57.049Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-18T11:25:57.049Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 90
acceptance_criteria: "The SDK exports a stable per-rule category map and a policy helper that decides a finding from a default plus partial overrides, typed so adding a rule never breaks a consumer that uses it; the SDK surface snapshot records the addition; release notes or the SDK migration notes call out new rule literals for exhaustive consumers; tests prove a newly added rule inherits the category default"
parent: pm-doxj
risk: low
confidence: medium
severity: medium
resolution: "Every SecretRule declares a required credential, network or path category. The SDK root and governance entrypoints export SecretGuardRule, the literal SecretGuardRuleCategory union, SECRET_GUARD_RULE_CATEGORIES, secretGuardRuleCategory and SecretGuardPolicy plus decideSecretGuardFinding (rule, then category, then default; own properties only; undefined counts as absent; malformed input throws TypeError; the decision type is inferred from the default). A compile-time check ties the SDK union to the rule inventory, and the surface snapshot records the literals. docs/MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md documents category policies, and docs/RELEASING.md documents contract_change declarations, including that a new detector is one."
expected_result: "A consumer that adopts the policy helper does not break when a detector is added, and the SDK offers a supported default-plus-overrides policy; an unchanged exhaustive Record over the rule union still needs migrating"
actual_result: "A category-only SecretGuardPolicy compiles whatever the rule union holds (type tests); a mocked future detector inherits its category decision (unit test); a rule this SDK does not know has no category, so an exact own rules entry for it decides it and otherwise the default does; inherited keys never decide anything, while an explicit own entry, even one named like a prototype member such as constructor, is an exact override; the release notes list the change under Changed (SDK contract) through its contract_change"
component: sdk
dependencies[2]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-hw00c2,related,"2026-11-17T21:58:37.940Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
  pm-x07qz2,related,"2026-11-17T21:58:37.940Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T09:42:40.269Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (branch feat/sdk-stable-consumer-classifications). Every SecretRule in src/core/shared/secret-rules.ts now declares a required category (credential, network or path), so a new detector cannot ship without one. The SDK (src/sdk/mutation-guard.ts, exported from the root, governance and runtime entrypoints) adds:\n- SecretGuardRule, an alias of SecretGuardFinding['rule'];\n- SecretGuardRuleCategory, the stable union;\n- SECRET_GUARD_RULE_CATEGORIES, a frozen map from rule to category;\n- secretGuardRuleCategory(rule), which returns undefined for an unknown or prototype name;\n- SecretGuardPolicy<Decision> = { default, categories?, rules? } and decideSecretGuardFinding(policy, finding).\n\nPrecedence: the rule entry, then the category entry, then the default. An undefined entry counts as absent, and null or false decisions are honoured. Only own properties are read, and keys that name no rule or category are ignored so that a policy written for a newer SDK still applies. A malformed policy or finding throws a TypeError.\n\nCategories: 17 rules are credential; private_ip, private_ssh_target and user_at_private_host are network; absolute_home_path is path. That reproduces the issue's example map exactly.\n\nSecretGuardFinding's signature is unchanged, so the surface snapshot records only additive symbols. Tests: tests/unit/sdk/security/secret-guard-rule-categories.spec.ts pins the full category map, the precedence and the TypeErrors, and mocks a future detector to prove it inherits its category decision unchanged. tests/types/secret-guard-policy.test-d.ts proves that a category-only policy compiles whatever the rule union holds and that unknown rule or category keys are type errors.\n\nDocs: docs/MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md has a new \"Deciding findings by category\" section. docs/RELEASING.md documents the contract_change declaration and states that a new detector is a declared contract change.\n"
notes[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T10:27:14.479Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on f0cbdfc88). The policy findings and how each was fixed:\n- P2: the policy's default, categories and rules were read even when inherited. Fix: decideSecretGuardFinding requires an own default, and reads categories and rules only as own properties. A test builds policies on a prototype.\n- P3: SecretGuardPolicy<Decision> allowed an undefined default whenever Decision includes undefined. Fix: default is Exclude<Decision, undefined>, with a type test.\n- P3: the docs said an unknown rule always takes the default. Fix: docs/MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md says an exact rules entry for it still applies, which is what the unit test already pinned."
  "2026-11-18T10:47:19.270Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on fd842f55f). The policy findings and how each was fixed:\n- P2: SecretGuardRuleCategory was an alias of the core SecretRuleCategory, so the SDK surface snapshot recorded only the alias name and could not detect a new category. Fix: the SDK spells the literal union out (\"credential\" | \"network\" | \"path\"). A compile-time two-way check fails when the rule inventory's category set drifts from it; the negative control (adding \"identity\" to SecretRuleCategory) fails tsc with TS2322. The snapshot now records the literals.\n- P3: the export test claimed the runtime entrypoint but imported the internal runtime-primitives barrel, which is not the published ./sdk/runtime. Fix: runtime-primitives is back to its main content, and the test claims only the root and governance entrypoints, which are where SecretGuardFinding is published."
  "2026-11-18T10:52:32.013Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1752 bot review, first pass: CodeRabbit on fd842f55f and Greptile. The policy findings and how each was fixed:\n- Greptile P2: with default typed as Exclude<Decision, undefined>, TypeScript could not infer Decision from the default, so an unannotated decideSecretGuardFinding({ default: true }, finding) returned unknown. Fix: default is Decision & ({} | null), which infers from the default. It still rejects undefined, including for SecretGuardPolicy<unknown> (CodeRabbit), and still allows null. Type tests cover the inferred boolean and string defaults, the unknown undefined default and a null default.\n- CodeRabbit: decideSecretGuardFinding read finding.rule and policy.default twice, so a getter could pass validation and then return something else. Fix: each is read once into a local. A getter test asserts a single read.\n- CodeRabbit: the future-detector test reused the statically imported guard when run alone with -t. Fix: vi.resetModules() before vi.doMock. It passes when run alone.\n- CodeRabbit: the runtime-entrypoint test claim was already fixed in c564fc892."
  "2026-11-18T11:25:36.337Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on 67c7eef6b). The policy findings and how each was handled:\n- P1: the default type Decision & ({} | null) failed @typescript-eslint/no-empty-object-type in the static gate. My eslint run had not covered mutation-guard.ts after that change. Fix: NonNullable<Decision> | Extract<Decision, null>. A tsc probe and the type tests confirm the same behaviour: default-only policies infer their decision type, undefined is rejected (including for SecretGuardPolicy<unknown>), and null stays a valid decision.\n- P3, declined with reason: a rules object assigned through a variable can carry extra keys whose values fail the Decision type. That is TypeScript's general structural-typing gap for non-fresh objects. Fresh literals still get excess-property checks, which catch typos (pinned by a type test), and an index signature would give up that typo check. At runtime only an exact own key decides."
  "2026-11-18T12:01:14.401Z","harness:claude-code","Codex round 6 and CodeRabbit's second pass, policy typing:\n- SecretGuardPolicy<unknown> rejected { default: null }, because Extract<unknown, null> is never. Fix: default is Decision & (NonNullable<Decision> | null). It still rejects undefined, still infers the decision type from a default-only policy, and has no {} literal for the lint rule to reject. Type test: SecretGuardPolicy<unknown> with a null default compiles.\n- Under exactOptionalPropertyTypes, an explicit undefined override entry was rejected, even though the contract treats it as absent. Fix: the categories and rules maps take Decision | undefined values; the default and the return type are unchanged. A tsc probe passes with and without exactOptionalPropertyTypes.\n- The stale snapshot (Greptile, CodeRabbit) was regenerated in e115066c4 and again now."
  "2026-11-18T14:05:29.392Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit's fourth full pass (on 29a0847a4), policy and record findings:\n- Major: a separately declared rules object could carry an extra key whose value is not a Decision, which a newer host's rule would then return. Fix: rules is typed { [shipped rule]?: Decision | undefined } & { readonly [rule: string]: Decision | undefined }, so every entry, including one for a rule this SDK does not ship, must be a decision. This matches the documented forward compatibility (a policy may already decide a newer host's rule). The trade-off: a misspelled rule key is no longer a type error. Type tests cover a forward rule entry and a mixed object assigned through a variable that is rejected.\n- Docs: the MUTATION_INTEGRITY example reviews findings with normalization_incomplete before applying category decisions. The doc states that the default must be defined (default: undefined throws; null is valid), and that a new detector takes its category's decision only when the policy names one, otherwise the default.\n- Records: the close reason, expected and actual results, and contract_change now say the helper protects consumers that adopt it. An unchanged exhaustive Record still breaks. A rule this SDK does not know is decided by an exact own rules entry, and otherwise by the default."
  "2026-11-18T19:01:47.256Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of 536c28739, P2: decideSecretGuardFinding inferred Decision only from the override entries when a policy was declared with satisfies (for example default refuse with categories network allow), because the default sits behind an intersection that TypeScript does not infer from. The policy parameter is now SecretGuardPolicy<Decision> & { default: Decision }; the plain default site makes inference take the union of the default and every override. tests/types/secret-guard-policy.test-d.ts covers a satisfies policy, inline mixed policies and inline null overrides. A negative control (old signature rebuilt into dist, which the type tests resolve through the package exports) reports TS2345 on the satisfies case. MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md no longer tells callers to annotate mixed-null policies. sdk/public-surface.json was regenerated from main's snapshot; the helper is new relative to main, so the only acknowledgement is still pm-5kfzjh's ManagedExtensionRecord one."
  "2026-11-18T19:12:59.905Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of d60036798, P2: the plain default inference site still failed for policies that mix decision kinds (default true with categories network allow, inline or with satisfies SecretGuardPolicy<true | \"allow\">), because TypeScript never unions candidates of different primitive kinds. decideSecretGuardFinding now infers the policy's own type (Policy extends SecretGuardPolicy<unknown>) and returns SecretGuardPolicyDecision<Policy>, a new exported type: the default plus every categories and rules entry, without undefined. Annotated SecretGuardPolicy<D> policies still return D. The call site still rejects a missing or undefined default and a categories map naming no stable category. Type tests cover mixed kinds inline and with satisfies, plus optional and unknown decision types. The api list of contract_change now names SecretGuardPolicyDecision. Greptile on 7af1369db: the hoisted readFileSync mock now answers this host's NSpid, so a process-tree test run alone sees the same default as the full file."
